-- title:
--   Lemma 10 — small-set boundary from vertex connectivity (as printed; see insight I2)
-- statement:
--   Fix the report generated by a correct $v$ in round $q$, with holders $I_r$, $k_r=|I_r|$, $u_r=|C_r\setminus I_r|$ and $b_r$ the number of uninformed correct vertices with an informed correct neighbour in $G_r$. At every round $r\ge q$ (after the report is generated): $k_r\ge1$; whenever $u_r>0$, $b_r\ge\min\{\rho,u_r\}$; and if $u_r\le\rho$ then $b_r=u_r$, where $\rho=t+2c+1$. This is stated exactly as printed; under Charon's finite retention the report expires at round $q+\Gamma$ (see insight I2), so the all-round claim is expected to be disprovable.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: I_r = correct vertices that hold this report immediately before the forwarding step of round r → correct processes of round r whose retained cache (after delivery, validation, expiry and own-report generation) contains a record with the report's payload; reason: copies and alternative signature encodings of one payload count once (§3.1), so holding the report means holding its payload
--   - reading: at every round after the report is generated → every round r ≥ q; reason: the report is generated in round q before forwarding and the source's proof starts from k_q ≥ 1
--   - reading: signatures existentially unforgeable against computationally bounded adversaries; results hold subject to this assumption (§2.5) → hypothesis E.NoForgery: every adversary-supplied record (initial or joining cache, Byzantine message) that verifies under a non-Byzantine identity carries that identity's genuine canonical report for a round in which it is present; reason: the source's correctness is conditional on signature security; this is that conditional abstraction, stated openly rather than as a cryptographic claim
--   - encoding: history-adaptive topology, churn and Byzantine adversary; arbitrary initial mutable states → universal quantification over all network sequences, all Byzantine message functions and all initial/joining caches; reason: the protocol is deterministic, so every adaptive strategy induces one such sequence; same set of executions
--   - reading: the prescribed maximum report encoding length (§3.1), with the same envelope bound for malformed records (§3.7) → the full binary record length is bounded by Crypto.maxReportBits(session, original round); ReportsFit requires each correct generated report to fit that public bound; reason: the source leaves the numerical format limit unspecified but uses it for whole envelopes; parameterizing it by signed context permits unbounded future identity and round encodings, while fixing the limit for every copy of a report; this asserts format compatibility, not delivery or complete evidence
-- source:
--   Sengupta and Perrin (2026), Byzantine Partition Detection Under Churn, supplied 48-page artifact (venue metadata: STOC 2027 submission; citation basis published per source.meta.json; SHA-256 3f9994406f38c73927d0b5cc78d0dd05740de0f7d09e544b7b203eefe39cdb74), Lemma 10, p. 21 (PDF 21)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CharonChurn_Main_Execution

namespace CharonChurn.Main

/-- Lemma 10 (Small-set boundary from vertex connectivity), p. 21, as printed. For the report
generated by correct `v` in round `q`, at every round `r ≥ q`: `k_r ≥ 1`; if `u_r > 0` then
`b_r ≥ min{ρ, u_r}`; and if `u_r ≤ ρ` then `b_r = u_r`, where `ρ = t + 2c + 1`. -/
theorem lemma_10 (E : Execution) (hE : E.Legal) (v q : ℕ) (hv : v ∈ correctSet E.N q) :
    ∀ r, q ≤ r →
      1 ≤ E.kInf v q r ∧
      (0 < E.uUninf v q r →
        min (E.P.t + 2 * E.P.c + 1) (E.uUninf v q r) ≤ E.bBdry v q r) ∧
      (E.uUninf v q r ≤ E.P.t + 2 * E.P.c + 1 → E.bBdry v q r = E.uUninf v q r) := by sorry

end CharonChurn.Main
